.34, "poll_margin": 11.33, "poll_sd": 4.83, "poll_weight": 0.55, "fundamentals": {"margin": 8.1, "lean": 2.25, "environment": 4.3, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 1.55}, "rating_margin": 12.5, "sigma_race": 4.5, "sigma_total": 4.93, "baseline_margin": 11.06, "analyst_adjustment": -0.06, "key_factors": ["Klobuchar's double-digit polling lead, averaging ~11 points across recent surveys", "Massive fundraising edge: $7.0M raised vs. $0.4M for Demuth", "Prediction markets price Klobuchar at 93-97% to win", "Minnesota's 3-point Democratic lean in an open seat"], "rationale": "Every recent poll shows Klobuchar ahead by 7 to 18 points, and the strongest surveys (KSTP/SurveyUSA, NYT/Siena, State Navigate) cluster around a 10-12 point margin. Her overwhelming financial advantage and near-unanimous expert ratings of Solid/Safe D leave little reason to expect a closer race.", "watch": "A late polling shift toward Demuth or unusually strong Republican turnout in the Iron Range and outer suburbs could narrow the margin, though not enough to threaten the outcome.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Tim Walz (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's open governor's race pits Senator Amy Klobuchar against state Representative Lisa Demuth after Tim Walz chose not to seek another term. Polling has been consistent all fall: Klobuchar led by 7 points in mid-September and by 10 to 18 points in the final week, with the best-regarded pollsters showing double-digit advantages. The polling average sits around 11 points, and prediction markets give Klobuchar a 93 to 97 percent chance of winning.\n\nThe resource gap is stark. Klobuchar has raised roughly $7 million and holds $3.9 million on hand, while Demuth has raised under $400,000. Expert raters unanimously place the race in the Safe or Solid Democratic column. Minnesota's modest Democratic lean and the absence of an incumbent on the ballot add only mild uncertainty; the evidence points to a comfortable Klobuchar victory